  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Thursday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A large upper level ridge of high\npressure over the southwest CONUS continues to situate near the\nfour corners high. This pattern is keeping high temperatures\nacross the region 10-15 degrees above normal for this time of\nyear. Various heat related products are in effect across the\nentire region and have been extended until 11 pm Sunday as high\ntemperatures remain in the upper 90s to 100s with Moderate to\nMajor heat risk indices across the area.\n\nTighter weather gradients to the north of Nevada will increase\nwesterly winds in the northern zones with speeds up to 15-20 mph,\ngusts as high as 20-25 mph. Elevated fire weather conditions will\nbe present tomorrow due to breezy winds along with low minimum \nrelative humidity and hot temperatures.\n\nTowards central Nevada, the high pressure over the four corners is\npushing monsoonal moisture back into the region. PWAT values,\nincrease in CAPE, and increased instability from high temperatures\nhas parts of central Nevada, in particular over White Pine \nCounty, will see dry thunderstorm development until the evening \nhours. These thunderstorms are expected to be short lived as storm\nmotion is slow, which will result with thunderstorm collapsing on\nthemselves as downdrafts will cut off the storm updrafts. Despite\nthe short lived storms, the greatest threat is still the potential\nfor dry lightning and strong gusty outflow winds from any\nthunderstorms reaching up to 40-50 mph.\n\nTomorrow, chances for more isolated dry thunderstorms across\ncentral Nevada as PWAT values and CAPE values are expected to be\nhigher than the day prior. Dry thunderstorms are expected across a\nwider area over White Pine County and Northeastern Nye County in\nthe afternoon. Critical fire weather conditions in the afternoon\nfor those areas due to the dry thunderstorm potential. A Red Flag\nWarning has been issued for NV fire zones 425 and 426 from 12 PM\nto 9 PM Saturday.\n\nSunday, monsoonal moisture will continue to move north into Nevada\nfrom the upper ridge with chances for monsoon thunderstorms to\nspread across central Nevada and reach into parts of northeastern\nNevada. Confidence remains low, but thunderstorms across central\nNevada expected to be a mix of wet/dry with little precipitation\nmaking it into the valleys.\n\nInto the next week, chances for monsoon thunderstorms each\nafternoon as the upper ridge is expected to stay over the four\ncorners and push moisture into Nevada. Much of the activity is\nexpected to stay over central Nevada and into parts of\nnortheastern Nevada. Temperatures are expected to be cooler with\nhighs in the 90s and low temperatures reaching back down into the\n50s. Extreme Heat Warning will still be in effect for eastern Elko\nCounty until 11 pm Monday as high temperatures stay in the upper\n90s, however low temperatures remain high in the upper 70s to 80s.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence continue for\nnear record heat across northern and central Nevada for the\nremainder of the week and into the weekend. Moderate confidence of\nelevated fire weather conditions due to hot, dry, and breezy winds\nover northern Nevada tomorrow. Moderate confidence of critical\nfire weather conditions in central Nevada due to dry thunderstorms\ntomorrow. Changes to the POP, weather, wind speeds, and wind gusts \ngrids were made to better reflect the oncoming monsoonal moisture\nand thunderstorms for today and tomorrow.",
